A||||2||mshill||Rebuilding 2.8 V6...||||11||
Z||000000||mshill||01-25-2000||04:53 PM||||I am in the process of rebuilding my 2.8L V6 myself.  I just got off the phone with the machine shop (who I consider to be competent and very reasonably priced) and my $150 estimated costs just went to about $300.<P>Initially, I took the block in to have the cylinders honed for pattern ($35), hot tanked ($25), cam bearings replaced ($20), I supplied the bearings) and oiled.  I also had the flywheel resurfaced ($15).  So, I have the block and flywheel ready to go for less than $100.<P>Then came the heads. I had them inspected and was expecting at a minimum a valve grind ($50 for both).  Turns out I need to have the valve guides replaced ($50 labor, $24 parts), two manifold bolt holes need helicoils ($12 labor, $3 parts) and the heads are slightly warped and need resurfacing ($25 each).<P>So, $300 for machine work (still not too bad), $110 for gaskets/seals/rings, $30 for a timing set, $100 for a cam/lifters, $25 for pushrods, comes to $565 and a lot of time disassembling, cleaning and reassembling the block. <P>Next time, I think I will just buy a long block.  It will be (at least for me) more cost/time effective.<P>Anyone else have similar experiences.||||reg||11
Z||000001||cobra427||01-26-2000||01:30 AM||||Hi,<BR>If you had your heads machined, it is also advisable to machine the lower manifold as well to make it fit properly on the heads.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||01:33 PM||||I had my 85 GT for less than a month when it spun a bearing. I decided it was better for me to open it up and see all of the lies that the previous owner told me. (LONG story). I had already been working on again and off again at Fiero Motorsport and Keith let me use his tools and space to rebuild my engine. I remember just taking everything off and having to just scrub it off from all the oil. I got laughs when I would be at the tank scrubbing off another piece of my engine. The laughs turned into admiration when they would be outside drying off.<BR>    Thats when it started. I call it "While-Im-At-It Syndrome." It started when I was getting tired of all of the sharp edges and cast flashes on the block and heads. Bill Wolff who at the time worked at FM told me how the old racers would "releive the block" by taking out all of the edges on the engine. Everything from water jacket edges to cast flashing on the outside (of course not the cyl. bores)got the edge taken off. The principal behind it is that microcracks that start in the flash can sometime work their way onto the block when it is stressed. Cracks do not start as well on a round surface as edged surface. Bill had already done a mountain of work on some heads for a turbo so I asked him to do the same magic on the valve head I had. Keith was working on the intercooled turbo so when he had reached the point for the cam to go to Babe Ersons, he asked me if I wanted my cam reground. Hey, I had it in my hand, "While-Im-At-It" I might as well get my cam reground for some mid range and sound. While-Im-At-It, I might as well paint the block and heads red. While Im at it I might as well pull my tranny out too and paint it red as well! While-Im-At-It syndrome got me triple chromed brackets, polished throttlebody, intake plenum and valve covers powdercoated and polished, urethane bushings for the engine cradle and the torque strut (after it was polished), 1:6 roller tip rockers, 3 angle valve grind job, high volume oil pump modification. It came together great and I still am proud of my work. Keith drove it around and remarked at the pick up it had. With that compliment I was on cloud 9. About a year later, we were just starting to put the first 3.4s in and were getting even more power. <P>Though I am still proud of my engine, next time Im going for the 3.4 long block. and even then I might take a year to play with it as well...||||reg||6
